From: ClypDat <noreply@clypdat.xyz>
Subject: [PATCH] decoder: a flush cancels a pending drain

input_DecoderDrain() is called when the demuxer reaches end-of-file, and only
sets b_draining; DecoderThread drains once its FIFO is empty. A seek arriving
before that point flushes the FIFO but left b_draining set, so the stale drain
ran as soon as the post-seek blocks were consumed. Draining flushes the codec,
which throws away its reference pictures: every following P-frame then decoded
against nothing and came out grey or ghosted until the next keyframe. Near the
end of a file there is no next keyframe, so playback stayed corrupt.

Seeking in the last second of a clip reliably triggers it, because the demuxer
reads ahead to end-of-file while prerolling, even when paused.

The drain request belongs to the stream position the flush discards.
---
 src/input/decoder.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

--- a/src/input/decoder.c
+++ b/src/input/decoder.c
@@ -2133,6 +2133,11 @@ void input_DecoderFlush( decoder_t *p_dec )
      * a row. */
     p_owner->flushing = true;
 
+    /* A drain requested before this flush was for the discarded stream
+     * position. Left pending, it would flush the codec right after the
+     * post-flush blocks are decoded, losing their reference pictures. */
+    p_owner->b_draining = false;
+
     /* Flush video/spu decoder when paused: increment frames_countdown in order
      * to display one frame/subtitle */
     if( p_owner->paused
